1
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.266
In the introduction,

2
00:00:03.266 --> 00:00:06.266
you learned a bit about what
function calling is.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.100
In this lesson,

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.166
we will describe it in more detail
and you will get some hands on experience.

5
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:13.366
Let's dive in.

6
00:00:13.366 --> 00:00:15.900
So what is function calling.

7
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.800
As described in the introduction.

8
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.000
Function-calling
is the capability of an LLM

9
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:26.033
to take in a natural language query,
along with a description of the function,

10
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.933
and output a string
that can be used to call that function.

11
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.066
Consider this example.

12
00:00:32.066 --> 00:00:36.066
You would like to know the temperature
in New York, and you have a function.

13
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:40.466
Get that that can provide you that value, but without function.

14
00:00:40.466 --> 00:00:44.566
Calling your LLM can help you out
with the function-calling the LLM.

15
00:00:44.766 --> 00:00:48.733
You provide the query and a description
of the function to the LLM.

16
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.500
The LLM has been trained to recognize
that it can use

17
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.200
functions as defined in the prompt
to answer the query.

18
00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:01.200
It will generate a string
that can be used to invoke that function.

19
00:01:01.766 --> 00:01:06.366
In this case, get them with the city
argument set to New York.

20
00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.100
You can now execute that function
and return the result

21
00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:12.866
and the query to the LLM.

22
00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:15.866
The LLM can now properly
answer the question.

23
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.766
Note here,
even though they're called function-calling

24
00:01:18.766 --> 00:01:22.166
LLMs,
they only generate a string.

25
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.566
They don't actually make the call.

26
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:26.466
You have to do that. Here,

27
00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:30.066
It's worthwhile to draw a distinction
between the general purpose

28
00:01:30.066 --> 00:01:33.066
LLMs
and the special-purpose LLMs.

29
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.833
General purpose
LLMs respond to all types of queries,

30
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.200
which can also include function
calling queries.

31
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.833
On the other hand,
special-purpose LLMs are fine-tuned

32
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:47.800
to focus on a single
or a small set of tasks.

33
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:53.033
An example of this is NexusRavenV2-13B,
which can be tuned to provide function-

34
00:01:53.366 --> 00:01:57.233
calling services, and will always try
to return a function call.

35
00:01:57.433 --> 00:02:02.433
Given a user query. Special purpose
LLMs can be smaller and offer better

36
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:07.833
latency than general-purpose LLMs.
Because they're fine-tuned for this task,

37
00:02:08.366 --> 00:02:11.433
They can often outperform
general purpose models

38
00:02:11.866 --> 00:02:15.166
for the tasks that they're trained for,
such as function calling.

39
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.166
Now, I've used a couple of different terms
here.

40
00:02:18.166 --> 00:02:21.233
Terms including function calling tools
and so on.

41
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.100
What is really the difference?

42
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.866
Well, function calling is the name
that's given

43
00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:31.300
to the LLM capability of forming the string
containing the function call.

44
00:02:31.366 --> 00:02:34.866
Tools are the actual functions
that are being called.

45
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:39.400
With that being said, let's make it
more concrete and let's build some tools.

46
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.200
Let's start by building local Python
tools.

47
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.166
Let's write
the tool that relies on matplotlib.

48
00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:49.233
It needs two inputs: x and y

49
00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:53.033
and plots the coordinates
that are specified by x and y.

50
00:02:53.666 --> 00:02:56.000
An example of this tool is,

51
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.833
if a user says that they want to plot
y is equal

52
00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:03.266
to ten x
for a certain set of values for x.

53
00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:07.033
You can use this tool to answer the user
query.

54
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.166
The way you would do
that is to write a function call

55
00:03:10.166 --> 00:03:12.233
that looks roughly like this.

56
00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:13.633
In this function call,

57
00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:17.033
you would pull out the details for x
is equal to one, two, three.

58
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.500
And because of the transformation function
requested,

59
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:25.400
you will map those values
to ten, 20, 30 on the y axes.

60
00:03:26.466 --> 00:03:27.133
While you

61
00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:30.633
can do
this, we want the LLM to do this for us.

62
00:03:30.966 --> 00:03:33.766
So the way you would do
this, is to provide the tool

63
00:03:33.766 --> 00:03:36.766
description
and the user query to the LLM.

64
00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:39.966
Let's start with the tool description.

65
00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:42.500
You start by providing the prototype

66
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.500
of the function you're using earlier.

67
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.400
Raven,

68
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.533
The LLM you'll be using, uses
Python formatted functions.

69
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:52.466
So you'll be utilizing that.

70
00:03:52.466 --> 00:03:56.000
You'll also add some descriptions
on what the tool does.

71
00:03:56.166 --> 00:03:59.800
This will tell the LLM what this function
or this tool

72
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.466
is meant to do,
and will improve its reasoning about

73
00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:07.733
if it should use this tool
to answer the user query or not.

74
00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:11.066
You will also provide the user
query to the LLM.

75
00:04:11.066 --> 00:04:14.833
Recall the user query was the plot
y is equal to ten x,

76
00:04:15.166 --> 00:04:17.866
which we can simply add here,
and then we're done.

77
00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:20.566
So this is essentially
our tool descriptions, right?

78
00:04:20.566 --> 00:04:23.566
You have provided a tool and a user query.

79
00:04:23.766 --> 00:04:26.800
You will call the function
call me an LLM named Raven.

80
00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:30.233
You can do this through the query
Raven function.

81
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.166
The result is a string.

82
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.100
The function name came from the function
prototype.

83
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:41.600
The arguments came from the user query,

84
00:04:41.766 --> 00:04:46.766
with the LLM actually doing some math
to generate ten times the inputs.

85
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.466
Cool.

86
00:04:48.466 --> 00:04:51.466
Now you can execute the string like this.

87
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.266
Great.

88
00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:55.200
Exactly what we expected.

89
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.766
Try this on your own.

90
00:04:56.766 --> 00:05:00.300
Here's the user query and a call
you can modify.

91
00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:03.633
Try changing the query
to produce different results.

92
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.800
So let's dive
deeper into what you just did.

93
00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:11.266
You told the LLM via the prompt

94
00:05:11.266 --> 00:05:14.866
how to format the Function
you've defined earlier

95
00:05:14.866 --> 00:05:17.866
by naming the functions
in a pythonic format,

96
00:05:18.266 --> 00:05:22.433
and you've also provided
in the form of pythonic arguments.

97
00:05:22.666 --> 00:05:26.633
The information you want
the LLM to extract from the user input.

98
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.733
you have also provided via
the description.

99
00:05:29.733 --> 00:05:34.733
The information necessary to understand
when a tool is relevant for a user query.

100
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.166
It's important to note that the LLM has
been trained to recognize function calls.

101
00:05:40.666 --> 00:05:43.866
This format is very specific
to such LLMs.

102
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.300
The LLM will respond to your user query

103
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.600
with a string
that you can use to call the function.

104
00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:53.900
Now let's take a look at more complicated
examples.

105
00:05:54.766 --> 00:05:57.633
Similar to earlier,
you will use a function

106
00:05:57.633 --> 00:06:00.900
that relies on matplotlib,
but you will make it do more.

107
