145
00:08:09.666 --> 00:08:13.100
that will allow you to kind of query
the OpenAI API.

146
00:08:13.333 --> 00:08:16.333
So the thing is, with the OpenAI API,

147
00:08:16.566 --> 00:08:21.033
you'll provide a description of the tool
and its arguments in a json format.

148
00:08:21.533 --> 00:08:24.266
While the description and the arguments

149
00:08:24.266 --> 00:08:27.900
will be identical to the pythonic format
you had earlier.

150
00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.200
The weights presented will be a bit
different.

151
00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.933
Please pause the video
and take a moment to compare the two.

152
00:08:35.966 --> 00:08:37.666
What do you notice in the response

153
00:08:37.666 --> 00:08:42.666
is that the attributes are in a format
that's not directly usable for us.

154
00:08:43.333 --> 00:08:46.433
Let's extract the function
in the arguments using the following

155
00:08:46.433 --> 00:08:49.433
approach.

156
00:08:51.566 --> 00:08:55.333
At the conclusion of this,
you get a Python call that you can now

157
00:08:55.366 --> 00:08:58.366
directly execute.

158
00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.400
With this,

159
00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.700
can see how you can utilize OpenAI's
function calling API

160
00:09:03.866 --> 00:09:07.966
to also build a cloud that addresses
the user query from earlier.

161
00:09:08.466 --> 00:09:12.400
What you did was actually bridged
the gap between the unstructured world

162
00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:13.800
of textual training data

163
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:18.133
that the LLM used in training,
with the highly structured world of code.

164
00:09:18.366 --> 00:09:22.233
In the next lesson, you will focus on
